before:
tryFrom returns the matching case or null for an unknown backing value. from instead throws ValueError for an unknown valid-type value. The conditional expression condition ? yes : no selects one result; here it chooses unknown for null and the backing value otherwise. The null check below makes rejection explicit; it is not a fabricated default status.

Status::from takes a backing value and returns the matching case, but throws ValueError for an unknown value of the valid scalar type. The companion handles that failure explicitly; unlike tryFrom, from does not return null for it.
```php
<?php
declare(strict_types=1);
enum Status: string { case Ready = 'R'; case Done = 'D'; }
echo Status::from('R')->value, "\n";
try { Status::from('X'); }
catch (ValueError $error) { echo "unknown\n"; }
```
Expected output: R; unknown.

Complete local PHP example; the app itself does not execute it:
```php
<?php
declare(strict_types=1);
enum Status: string { case Ready = 'R'; case Done = 'D'; }
$status = Status::tryFrom('D');
echo $status === null ? 'unknown' : $status->value, "\n";
```

